    <!-- A Super 32X game with two sound makers. The music of the Mega Drive side is made by a sound driver that the game copies from the
         cartridge (0xE0000 in the file) into the 68000's RAM at 0xFF4000: three entry points (+0 once a frame, +4 start, +8 the call that takes a function number
         in D0 and a parameter in D1; function 1 puts the number of a song, 0x80 and up, into the driver's request queue; the game sends this driver nothing else, and a number below 0x80 makes it read data that is not a song). The
         driver's work area is at 0xFF5D16 (the pointer to its song table, which the game sets, is at +0x20). The sampled sounds (the voices, the hits) are
         played by the slave SH-2 through the 32X's PWM unit: a track with unit="pwm" is the game's own number of a sample, which the Machine puts into
         the slave's queue (the way the master does) and then tells the slave to play. The music of the Mega Drive side has sampled sounds too (the drums):
         the driver leaves their numbers in two words of its work area (sh2_mailbox, work area + 4 and + 6; the number is the low byte), which the game's own code hands to the master
         once a frame, and the Machine does the same. -->

